  "answer": "I'll start by reading the project skills that apply to this cart-limit change, then look through the storefront cart API.The request already specifies the limit, the config key, the default, and the 400 response. I\u2019ll follow test-driven development and look through the storefront cart API and merchant configuration next.The cart endpoints all go through one facade. I\u2019ll add a failing test for the per-store line limit, then enforce it before the cart is saved.The test failed because the limit type does not exist yet. I\u2019ll add that type and call it before any cart write.The storefront cart now rejects any add or quantity change that would leave a line with more units than the store's `CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY` merchant setting (99 when that setting is absent). The limit is read on each request, a quantity of 0 still removes the line, and an over-limit request returns HTTP 400 without changing the cart. This covers creating a cart, updating one line, and updating several lines at once.\n\nVERIFICATION: {\"ran\": [\"mvn -pl sm-shop -am test -Dtest=CartLineQuantityPolicyTest -DfailIfNoTests=false\"], \"result\": \"passed\", \"could_not_verify\": [\"HTTP 400 from a running storefront and that an existing cart row is unchanged after a rejected update\"], \"missing\": [\"an integration test that saves CART_MAX_LINE_QUANTITY and calls POST /api/v1/cart, PUT /api/v1/cart/{code}, and POST /api/v1/cart/{code}/multi\"]}",
